WEBVTT

00:00:01.290 --> 00:00:02.254
Hi, I'm Plum!

00:00:02.254 --> 00:00:06.434
A Yale researcher packaged his paper-plotting style as a skill for coding agents.

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:09.649
Here's how it's built, and what its own figures show.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:14.224
Commit each script beside its data and the figure it made, and

00:00:14.224 --> 00:00:17.035
a house style becomes checkable, not a matter of taste.

00:00:18.467 --> 00:00:20.369
An agent reads the skill file first.

00:00:20.369 --> 00:00:23.630
It's a router: when to use the style, when not to, and

00:00:23.630 --> 00:00:24.717
which file to open.

00:00:25.137 --> 00:00:27.497
Five reference files hold the rules.

00:00:27.497 --> 00:00:32.215
The design theory file is almost all numbers: font sizes, line widths,

00:00:32.215 --> 00:00:33.788
resolution, nine hex codes.

00:00:34.209 --> 00:00:36.460
The canonical examples are the figure folders.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:40.961
Each keeps its script, its data and the image it produced, side by side.

00:00:41.381 --> 00:00:43.721
One file specifies nine helper functions.

00:00:43.721 --> 00:00:48.792
None exist, so every agent session writes its own, and the consistency slips.

00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.880
Drop the top and right spines.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:52.786
They encode nothing.

00:00:53.206 --> 00:00:54.973
Paint your own method one dark blue.

00:00:55.915 --> 00:00:58.527
Greens for improvements, reds for baselines.

00:00:58.527 --> 00:01:01.138
Under deuteranopia, that pair nearly merges.

00:01:01.558 --> 00:01:03.264
And print the value on every bar.

00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:06.478
One rule is missing.

00:01:06.478 --> 00:01:10.213
The headline figure starts its y-axis at zero point five, so a

00:01:10.213 --> 00:01:11.769
bar's height isn't its score.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:14.774
The two scores differ by one point six four times.

00:01:15.236 --> 00:01:16.616
Subtract the floor from both,

00:01:18.088 --> 00:01:20.119
and the drawn bars differ ten times.

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:21.860
A bar's length is its encoding.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.375
A style guide for agents needs a checker, not a spec, and

00:01:26.375 --> 00:01:28.012
a bar's length is its encoding.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:34.750
To recap: commit the outputs, route the agent, and make the rules a checker.

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:37.610
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:38.212
I'm Plum.

00:01:38.212 --> 00:01:38.514
Bye!
